ftware  it could be reviewing it could be  interacting with other things in the  world and from looking at the  transcripts of what it communicates with  the rest of the world you could learn  information from it so the the real  notion of you see doesn't just consider  input-output behavior it also considers  these kinds of side effects and forces  them to be part of your modeling process  so your hope is that your complicated  computer program is represented by this  specification both in the way that it  interacts with the rest of the world  both in terms of how the rest of the  world sees it from from an input/output  semantics and in terms of any side  effects it spews out into the world okay  so you want to prove that these two  things are close so actually you don't  want to prove that they're identical you  want to prove that the hope is that the  specification is simpler to understand  than the program so you hope that  there's some sort of translation  mechanism or a simulator in the crypto  parlance that can translate between them  and so this is how how we'll define  security in the notion in the language  of Universal composability that's some  complicated program is is is is somehow  it is as secure as if we use some sort  of simpler to understand specification  if they're functionally equivalent P and  s are function equivalent and there's  some way to translate from all of the  side effects that you see in the real  world into all of the side effects that  you see in the specification and so the  hope is that the specification is some  simpler representation of the program  and so this was like a super quick  description of this complicated notion  of Universal composability if you're  interested in seeing more information  about it we have several hours of  lectures by Ron that we've recorded and  put on YouTube okay so in this in this  project this modular approach to cloud  security project are one of our big  objectives has been to prove the UC  